#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221169 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "6649e376-1865-5cb9-8892-687f80082d22" :question/math-academy-id "e-21650" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2=-5,$ with corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [3, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-2, \\ 1]^T,$ respectively. Using this information, sketch the phase portrait of the system." :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n![](images/cf/cfff34f683e4c790f0f669a1babde2e9da9fcc10769c674f037ed3328d658f9f.png)\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ![](images/75/75c8006b52acb68b62c4f827fdd7b8c08709c647c7994f3088bf4f8f72eb3b8f.png) are the equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac13x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c39f92243806a8682701374244fa2f7def32ab56ae509c920a31e75abfacb88f.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/23/236a2688de746c23166d9159dfc073781ed55bc33cb289230f8bc9dbc00e323d.png) has the equation \n$y=- \\dfrac12x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/25/2527c2ffbb46a109eac9386b6b4bb634ca98768cf261252a2b94daf72f11a4a0.png)\nlie on that line. Since $e^{-5t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n![](images/96/96a198c7fe3d98d3775ffc176b6659022b18676caf849cbc8d5c66833113bb19.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/74/74b573da705aafba25c74aa789dc3a51464683dd5a47a999ba9c557d35ed1d02.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221170 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4dd289f-95e4-5d51-8544-d20e3f5b158c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "e89c64cb-e046-592c-929b-9269cb7b2758" :question/math-academy-id "e-21651" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+9y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\mathbf{v} = [3, \\ 1]^T.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [-1, \\ 0]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$  Sketch the phase portrait of the system." :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n![](images/32/32cdfec727ba37d7c21b4df53d40d29853c56a0bf7c5469227882de8bceb735a.png) \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3t-1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/81/810d48ff45c63e14ede1c9b7742298b263c861bba689595af6fe7afeac69f96d.png) are equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac13x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bfc7b00f7f63aec120179da3f89f72f0aa53f13d299c95ed8f53e6238d329e51.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n![](images/93/93ec8854dd09842895e8f0854683a03c2cae129198802f9b49c3d426179eb78b.png)\nFor example:\n\n- When $c_1=1$ and $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3t+2 \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/55/55dfdfce480deba74fe46c1634e4c7ffb711fbd617525cdb5387bdabfc83ae75.png)\n\n- When $c_1=-1$ and $c_2=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-3t-2 \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/2f/2fb2297c741ea20ca729dcacb9934d7f8d1cca58af93c2b75db177890934b2b6.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9e/9e7970ba2d6bbe4745ee498cdbc72f2b7d8689219291847644bd6483696cc456.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221171 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e50fd4a2-76cd-5de5-b461-28a07c4f98bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/id #uuid "edb1633f-44e1-5324-8048-80252c65afe5" :question/math-academy-id "e-21654" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/1e/1e5a8eef63597c74566c624df6f52cf446c13d6d0ae16612b5ac3c45479f8816.png)\nwhere ![](images/91/9169c7aaf58e872a751777fffb26d435573ece6d79b1af64ccbe4b1bf6544463.png)\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+2y=0 \\\\ 5x+5y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+2y=0\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n2y=-2x\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n![](images/72/72ee846ffe127bc7d4fb9e7b37b633f2c07e71f439c18471792e05fb56d0f7e1.png)\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0adb1db8-6564-5d3a-98a9-63a8577d6a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8a/8a178460d4e87756694e03bc37dd58c3e70cf3222389b31fe838aeb6a910b984.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302530} {:answer/id #uuid "4380720f-1f8d-546c-8c6c-7f3e30b7ae37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1bf19c8cc89bd745c5515570921e1db00b552d351459ccd2e17921d63c4fa3d7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302882} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3bbaf7-df7d-566b-bc9f-5d780f15b3a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2e/2e18d0b6def07666f9eaf4f4d544b8c58c5fc87675f7b7785a1fb71d3951272b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303626} {:answer/id #uuid "e8434b45-7886-5763-9997-1e6b539ae716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303900} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b0ecfb-c339-5814-ac36-fff1b89477a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/69/690295c0ea992de3c2a484c377ddeb0750f8438d16a30681eb6f8cc81b484946.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9b0ecfb-c339-5814-ac36-fff1b89477a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/69/690295c0ea992de3c2a484c377ddeb0750f8438d16a30681eb6f8cc81b484946.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304019} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9e34509-f550-5d56-98a1-f961132ada15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "059b4cf7-aecd-509d-b95b-c42c513302b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-332864" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/80/80798b43ad4d439adbe0bdc827f5e1fed64de0a63660c7bca0b610b49445d859.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/69/690295c0ea992de3c2a484c377ddeb0750f8438d16a30681eb6f8cc81b484946.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e50fd4a2-76cd-5de5-b461-28a07c4f98bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c00eaa1-4277-537d-bcf2-07dd2d71aea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\text{ only}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305254} {:answer/id #uuid "470597d4-3f64-5957-8477-e42303e5cdd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305841} {:answer/id #uuid "848ed6e4-8b33-52af-b15f-3912ade2742c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{ only}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306414} {:answer/id #uuid "b3666def-622f-5d29-b763-322e54d9efe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306834} {:answer/id #uuid "ee88a534-7d05-5f3f-8c60-c3935e9b3a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2k \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "470597d4-3f64-5957-8477-e42303e5cdd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305841} :answer-field/id #uuid "eec63233-efc6-5468-9d0d-3bdbfeffc991" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85e6fc84-64ed-533c-94e3-94bfdcc415ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-333782" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=0 \\\\ 4x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one:\n\n$$\n2(2x+y)=4x+2y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05542e74-e525-5cb7-8e46-a744cdb6e492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f0/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305199} {:answer/id #uuid "a28748a2-30b9-5993-b947-2209f567754d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dd/dd3667299cd5da8fe5d9450799fbe865e832449ac39427795a5b87cf2786f587.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306678} {:answer/id #uuid "a46b67c1-b13d-5ae8-957a-a664665034d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8a/8a7b634d05a938d9b481620da9837a8f28dc28f84ac18a3f9a20678ead0df20b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306700} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2d227a-1ddc-5b0b-82ec-39ad7fba9c98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f4/f4b88938a280acb1149be29d6832483713437236424bbb640784a55bafea712c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306758} {:answer/id #uuid "da4b3bdf-1f79-5acb-9f40-db982a1bfa54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/59/59b96e2396db2559e9893c73ef530a312a347442b9c4fde0137583310ceba0a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05542e74-e525-5cb7-8e46-a744cdb6e492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f0/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305199} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b46cd2e-0392-50d7-bcaf-b0944ec48370" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b9dc234-d83d-52ed-858a-43cf39227d57" :question/math-academy-id "q-333832" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18fad31b9f69e55d9f4bc414d3e1b732eaee7685bf36a183033fc302e911eb82.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=-3x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/2b/2bfda58fee727ccdc9f424140e2b8143bf5afe426ef0b1fa72105272d9a1b41e.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f0/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4dd289f-95e4-5d51-8544-d20e3f5b158c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fecab66-dba7-588d-b051-c89c33672842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7b/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305295} {:answer/id #uuid "4c93c30d-56ef-5dbb-a2c3-12f0c02b1cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3a/3aa0329f2b3017616c2010d4674c0dd13d9e277abe790e276a4e294087d6c685.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305891} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0da9f4-1a35-5132-acee-bff9e95cc05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4a/4a8d9528c878089370476a45aab127a416a11595ea0b0f972708e5161f208d5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306355} {:answer/id #uuid "8a151970-06b2-5c13-989f-71d57aa7a478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/27610876df35c3b1e8e67e8958a5f28e26dfc26262e55994277629789a4bbd84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306471} {:answer/id #uuid "9c1b08d6-6709-5e77-8f04-4e1eb9a5d4f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/15/15f6170ed7aa922fb1be05914229dd91d98c64879050b15b2302839d419e5177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fecab66-dba7-588d-b051-c89c33672842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7b/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305295} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e4c7528-85a6-56a0-a678-684c0edfdcb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2fbf3cd8-344d-52ae-9b3c-567d393e20b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-334638" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-9x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }3]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[-\\frac{1}{3},\\text{ }0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a70188cf5de7e7b64530aa661447b7fa7fc1e918ca3de41088e67274d813b7a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3t \\\\ y=9t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/37/37deb5a0fda7ef15a016ec2d73a3b935074b6696184dd480ebe5b858ce517500.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-3t \\\\ y=-9t-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/60/60b49680cf94888122f25432586a3546dea206ae960f951afd187444afc8881a.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7b/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png)"}]}
